Referee report on `reports/subexp_pathwidth.md` §5 and `learning/subexp_chain.py` (checked against the file as it stood during this session — it was being edited live: "679 of 679" became "995 of 995" and the §5.1/§5.7 incidence formula became `Θ(n³ + n² k + n m)` while I worked; both edits agree with my findings).

**Mathematical statements**

1. **Lemma 5.1 — SOUND.** `∂_in S = ∂(V ∖ S)` and the reversal bijection are correct; the `n = 1` edge case (empty product, count 1, `vs = 0`) is consistent. My own permutation loop (outer-boundary prefixes), my own `vs_of_layout` count, `chain_count_dp` outer and inner, and `C_k > 0 ⟺ vs ≤ k` agree on all 251 (graph, k) pairs: atlas to 5 plus `K_2..K_6`, zero failures; `C_k(K_n) = n!` for `k ≥ n − 1` and 0 below, `n = 2..6`.

2. **Theorem 5.2 — SOUND**, one cosmetic gap. Every auxiliary is functionally determined: `x` by `y`; `o` by `y` (for `N(u) = ∅` the clause `[-o]` is the unit `o ↔ false`, correct); `b` by `y, o`; each `r[i][j]` by one of six case-split equivalences, all of which I checked are exactly `r ↔ a ∨ (x ∧ b)` with the right None conventions (`prev[0]` = true, `prev[j]` = false for `j > i`). The `b = None, a ≠ None` branch is unreachable but harmless. Model count equals my own permutation count on all 283 (graph, k) pairs of the atlas to 5 *including `k = n`* (where `k + 1 > n`, no unit clause), and on `n = 1, 2`, isolated vertices, `(0,1)+(2,3)`, `K_3 + K_1`: zero failures. Gap: the statement says "the unit `¬r[t,n,k+1]`" unconditionally; for `k ≥ n` that variable does not exist (`s[k+1] is None`) and the clause is absent — vacuous, but the statement should say so.

3. **§5.1 headline "in `Θ(n²)` variables" — WRONG as stated** (one-line answer at line ~1557 and the §5.1 heading). The module's own formula is `4n(n−1) + (n−1) Σ_i min(i+1, k+1) = Θ(n² k)`: at `n = 125, k = 24` the counter supplies 350,300 of the 412,300 variables (85%), the `y` only 15,500. The same paragraph later says `N = Θ(n² k)`. `Θ(n²)` would need a binary adder, which the text says is "not implemented". Also "R3 holds up to the paper's own branching" is not true of the implemented pairwise at-most-one: every one of the `n²` `x` variables sits in `n − 1` AMO clauses, far more than the `n/√log n` variables the paper branches on; it holds only for the ladder AMO, which again is not implemented.

4. **Lemma 5.3 — SOUND.** Degree `d + 1`, `2^{d+1} − 2` monomials, top coefficient `(−1)^d`: verified on all 231 vertices of the atlas to 5 (including degree-0 vertices, where the polynomial is 0), zero failures.

5. **Lemma 5.4 — SOUND.** Top coefficient `(−1)^{n−k} C(n−1, k)` verified by Möbius transform for all `n ≤ 9`, every `k`. Rank bound `≤ min(|A|, |B|, k) + 1` verified on all 168 (n ≤ 7, k, |A|) splits, zero violations (tight on 155). The proof's "at most `min(|A|, k) + 1` and at most `|B| + 1`" is correct since distinct rows are the nested indicators `[j ≤ c]`.

6. **Lemma 5.5 — SOUND.** The `last(v)` convention handles isolated vertices correctly (`last = pos`, never counted); the 0-based `t = 0..n−2` indexes prefixes of size `1..n−1`, matching Kinnersley. Verified on every layout of the paw, `P_5`, `P_3 + 2K_1`, `K_4 − e + K_1`, `K_2 + K_1`: 1,524 cuts, zero failures, and `max_t` equals `vs_of_layout` on every layout.

7. **Lemma 5.6 — SOUND**, but see item 13.

8. **Lemma 5.7 — SOUND**, and the predicate in `sequences_count` is consistent with the statement (the `T2 == full` branch is redundant since `V ∈ L_k`; "prefix supports for `1 ≤ t ≤ n`" matches the DP). The stall identity `W_k(S) = Σ_j c_j(S) h_{n−j}(1..j)`, which the module does not check, holds on all 6,258 (graph, k, S) triples of the atlas to 5, with `c_j(S)` from my own chain enumeration; the Möbius sentence `Σ_S (−1)^{n−|S|} c_j(S) = [j = n] C_k` holds on all 1,302 (graph, k, j) triples.

9. **§5.6 tensor-rank fact — SOUND** given Boolean wires (`rank ≤ 2^w` is the standard cut argument; with `d`-dimensional wires it is `d^w`, which the text should say since R6 allows "large local tensors").

10. **§5.6 measurement — WRONG in part, by a code bug.** `balanced_splits` yields only `A` with `0 ∈ A, |A| = ⌊n/2⌋`. For even `n` that is every unordered balanced partition, but for odd `n` the splits with vertex 0 on the large side are *different partitions* and are skipped: 15 of 35 at `n = 7`, 4 of 10 at `n = 5` (my count). Recomputed over all `C(n, ⌊n/2⌋)` splits: `n = 7, vs − 1`: max over graphs of `min log₂ rank` is **2.58, not 2.81**; "graphs above the threshold's bound" is **87%, not 90%**; `n = 7, vs`: **68%, not 75%**. Medians unchanged. The sentence "two graphs have full rank 8 across every balanced split at `k = vs = 2`: `C₇` and a 6-cycle with a pendant" is **wrong for the second graph**: edges `[(0,3),(0,4),(1,2),(2,5),(2,6),(3,6),(4,5)]` has rank 7 on one of the 20 skipped splits; only `C₇` is full on all 35. `n = 4, 5, 6` rows: `n = 5` numbers happen to be unchanged except the full-rank count. The "two graphs" claim and the two `n = 7` rows need regenerating, and `split_rank.csv` is wrong for `n = 5, 7`.

11. **§5.7 "so it costs at least `n` incidence in any system in which the bits it reads determine the set … so every such system has incidence `≥ n(n−1)`" — GAP.** "Essential in all `n` set variables" gives `≥ n` scope only when the test's inputs *are* the `n` set bits of that cut (nested/position encodings). "Bits that determine the set" is weaker: a `t`-set is determined by `⌈log₂ C(n, t)⌉ < n` bits, so the argument as written yields `Σ_t log₂ C(n,t) ≈ n²/(2 ln 2) ≈ 0.72 n²` — still `Θ(n²)`, but not the constant `n(n−1)`. The premise itself is measured, not proved (995 of 995 connected atlas graphs at `k = vs − 1`, which I reproduced; and 704 of 704 random connected graphs at `n = 8–10` in my probe), and §5.9 says so, but §5.7 states the conclusion as a theorem.

12. **§5.7 "the three on record have `Θ(n³ + n² k + n m)` as counted (`Θ(n² + n m)` with the standard gadgets), and `Θ(n² log n)`" — now SOUND** (the pre-edit `Θ(n² k + n m)` was wrong: the pairwise AMO is 1,922,000 of the 6,165,523 incidence on the ridge row, 31%).

13. **§5.7 "The exact count … cannot sit below `n log₂ n − 1.44 n` variables in any unweighted system (Lemma 5.6)" — GAP.** Lemma 5.6 is a statement about `(K_n, k = n − 1)`, a trivially-yes instance; a system for a graph with `C_k(G) = 1` (or `0`, every refutation level) needs no such bound. The sentence should say "on `K_n`" / "for a scheme that handles every graph". Same for §5.4 "no within the verified budget for any `n ≥ 11`" (correct as arithmetic: `log₂ 11! = 25.3 > B_v(11) = 22.3`; at `n = 10` it is `21.79 < 21.81` and needs integrality, which is presumably why it was changed from 10 to 11).

14. **Smallest-example paragraph — SOUND**, one loose word. `forward_layers(paw, k_mosp = 2)` gives `[1, 1, 0]`, and of the six connected 4-vertex graphs the paw is the only one with a nonzero second layer at `k = vs − 1`. `K_{1,6}` + leaf edge, `vs = 2`, `forward_layers(k_mosp = 2)` gives `[1, 4, 6, 4, 1, 0]` exactly as stated. "One ordering survives the first cut" should be "one prefix set" (six orderings start at the pendant). "`Θ(n)` of the `n − 1` cut tests are consulted and none can be dropped" is rhetoric: nothing in §5 proves that an intermediate cut before the wall cannot be dropped (that is Lemma 4.6's territory, which is cited only for coarse chains).

**Table arithmetic**

15. §5.1 `n = 125` rows: `cnf_size_formula(125, [2m//125]*125, k)` reproduces all four rows exactly (412,300 / 2,529,598 / 6,165,523; 694,400 / 3,716,774 / 9,441,727; 886,600 / 4,606,474 / 11,915,527; 1,001,300 / 5,775,174 / 15,303,827); `15,500 / 110.8 = 140` ✓. §5.4: `log₂ n!` and `B_v = n √ln n / (ln ln n)²` reproduce every row to the printed precision (8: 15.3 / 21.5; 10: 21.8 / 21.8; 16: 44.3 / 25.6; 32: 117.7 / 38.6; 64: 296.0 / 64.3; 125: 695.2 / 110.8; 10³: 8,529 / 704; 10⁶: 1.85e7 / 5.39e5), ranks column ✓. The `n log₂ n − 1.44 n` constant is `log₂ e` ✓. The K₃ indicator polynomial in §5.2 evaluates correctly on all five members.

